---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Method"
      eyebrow="How to read this archive"
      dek="Every claim here carries a label saying how well it is known. The labels are the point: an archive that presents testimony and documents in the same voice is not a history, it is a story with footnotes.">


  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The five labels</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">Label</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span></td>
          <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan. Most of this archive's
            documented claims trace to <strong>Dutch Reformed Church registers</strong> and to
            <strong>Cape and Transvaal probate records</strong>, cited by FamilySearch ark.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span></td>
          <td>A reasoned conclusion drawn from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be
            overturned. The cousin marriage, the reading of the surname distribution, and the identification
            of the 1820 Settler are all inferences.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span></td>
          <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because pretending
            otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip inf">Superseded</span></td>
          <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="chip" style="border-color:var(--terra);color:var(--terra)">Disputed</span></td>
          <td>Asserted somewhere in the record and contradicted somewhere else in it. Every one is listed.
            <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The living are left out</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Living people are omitted from this build entirely</b> — not hidden, not gated, not present in
      the output.</p>
    <p>Because this line reaches the present within three generations of Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen,
      that removes a large part of the story from public view. <b>Cheryl Anne Lerena</b>, David
      Defranceski's mother, appears only as a name and a birth year where the descent cannot be explained
      without her. Her siblings' living descendants do not appear at all.</p>
    <p>A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Where this one starts</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Unusually well. The <b>Lerena</b> archive began with almost nothing documented. This one begins with
      a MyHeritage tree of <b>15,643 people</b> in which the Booyzen line is already carried back
      <b>seven generations</b>, and in which a large proportion of births, marriages and deaths already
      carry a <b>FamilySearch citation to an original register</b>.</p>
    <p>That changes the job. The work here is not to find the family. It is to <b>test what has been
      assembled</b> — because a tree that good will be believed, and parts of it are wrong.</p>
    <p>So this archive did three things the tree does not do:</p>
    <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;padding-left:22px">
      <li><b>Listed every contradiction</b> rather than choosing between them silently.</li>
      <li><b>Tested the family testimony against outside evidence</b> — surname distribution, Cape Rebel
        district figures, the 1820 Settler record — instead of repeating it.</li>
      <li><b>Named what has not been searched</b>, so the gaps are visible.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="controlled-negative">A nil return is worth nothing until you show the search works</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This is the thing this archive actually does, and until today it was not written down anywhere.
      It arrived by being wrong six times in a week.</p>
    <p><b>An index that returns nothing is making one of two statements and it does not tell you which.</b>
      Either the record does not exist, or your search could not have found it. Those are opposite
      conclusions and they look identical on the screen. So a negative is not publishable until something
      has been run through the same search that is <em>known to be there</em> &mdash; a control &mdash;
      and come back.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">The nil return</th><th>What it actually meant</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>&ldquo;148 of 184 &mdash; thirty-six not reached&rdquo;</b></td>
            <td class="cap">All ten pages <em>had</em> been walked. <b>This archive's own harvesting code</b> required a description longer than three characters and silently dropped every row the index leaves short. A caveat that blamed the source for a hole this archive dug.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>A register with no Barrys in it</b></td>
            <td class="cap">The <b>Name column was blank</b> for a whole class of entry. A filter that drops what it cannot see is indistinguishable from an archive that holds nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>BARRY and BURGERSDORP: 6,245 documents</b></td>
            <td class="cap">More than BARRY alone. <b>NAAIRS treats two keywords as OR</b> unless the operator is sent. A search that returns more than its own first term is not a search.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>&ldquo;LUUS with BOOYZEN returns nothing&rdquo;</b></td>
            <td class="cap">It returns nothing <b>from the estate index</b>, which was the only index asked. A marriage transcription holds the marriage. The sentence did not say what had been asked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>No Kolbes, Mountjoys or Sliers in the Cape inventories</b></td>
            <td class="cap">That index <b>runs from the 1780s to the 1830s</b> and all three families reached the Cape in the nineteenth century. A zero from an index that ends before your people arrive is not a finding.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Nothing older than the 1840s, for weeks</b></td>
            <td class="cap">Every register in the filmed church collection <b>begins in 1843</b>. The registers themselves go back two centuries. It was the edge of a filming project, and it announced itself nowhere.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>So the rule is: run a control, and publish it with the negative.</b> When this archive reported
      that James Mountjoy is not among the discharge papers at Kew, it published alongside that
      <b>ninety-five records for his regiment in the same series in a single page of results</b> &mdash;
      because the number that makes the absence meaningful is not his, it is theirs.</p>
    <p>The same search also turned up the reason the control mattered. The catalogue indexes the regiment
      as <b>&ldquo;21st Dragoons (Light)&rdquo;</b>, not as the newspaper wrote it and not as this archive
      had been writing it. <b>One word order between a real record and a nil return.</b></p>
    <blockquote>A search that has not been controlled has not been done. It has only been attempted, and
      the difference between <b>&ldquo;searched and not found&rdquo;</b> and <b>&ldquo;could not
      search&rdquo;</b> is the most important distinction on this site.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">Which is why <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read</a> marks a row
      <b>void</b> when the search itself was faulty, and <b>blocked</b> when it could not run &mdash; and
      why a build here now refuses if a blocked row does not say what would unblock it. A negative with no
      control behind it is an opinion with a citation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="question-nobody-asked">Each was a correct answer to a question nobody asked</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This one was named on <b>21 September 2026</b> by the session keeping the D&rsquo;Arcy archive,
      after the two of us had between us produced six of them in a day. It is the rule
      <a href={u("/method/#controlled-negative")}>the controlled negative</a> does not reach, because
      <b>half of these never returned a nil at all.</b> A test passed. A count came back. A confidence
      field said <em>exact</em>.</p>
    <p><b>An instrument can be working perfectly and be pointed at something else.</b> What it reports is
      then true, and about the wrong thing, and it arrives wearing exactly the face of an answer. A nil at
      least announces itself as bad news worth checking. <b>A confident yes does not.</b></p>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">What it reported</th><th>What it was actually describing</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>&ldquo;345 reordered, zero content changed&rdquo;</b></td>
            <td class="cap">A comparison that iterated the dossier <b>dict&rsquo;s keys</b> &mdash; n, slug, mentions, pages, hits &mdash; which are identical for every person alive or dead. <b>The reassuring answer was the only answer it could return.</b> 345 dossiers had in fact changed content.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Two byte-identical runs of the generator</b></td>
            <td class="cap">Both runs read <b>the same built site</b>, and the generator&rsquo;s input was 81% its own previous output. It proved the disk had not changed between two reads of it. <b>Generate, rebuild, generate</b> moved all 347.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>&ldquo;0 results for Westfalen&rdquo;</b></td>
            <td class="cap"><b>Seven of the eleven records on the page were Westphalian.</b> FindMyPast&rsquo;s place filter is not applied to that collection at all, so the zero described the filter and never touched the data.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Three empty full-text searches</b></td>
            <td class="cap"><b>HTTP 000 &mdash; no connection.</b> The endpoint no longer exists. Three nil returns that described a hostname.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>36 map pins stamped <code>exact</code></b></td>
            <td class="cap">The confidence field recorded that <b>the lookup succeeded</b>, not that it answered the question. Twenty-one places sat on the geocode for the word <b>&ldquo;England&rdquo;</b> &mdash; right country, wrong precision, and <b>a wrong-country gate passes it cleanly</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>A gate reporting a clean build</b></td>
            <td class="cap">It had been run <b>before the row that broke it existed</b>, and on checks that do not touch that schema. <b>A build that did not cover the change is not a build.</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>The test that catches all six is the same one, and it is one question:</b> if the thing I am
      looking for were definitely there, could this instrument have told me? Ask a filter for something
      already seen with your own eyes on the page in front of you. Ask a comparison to fail on a
      difference you planted. Ask a generator to survive a rebuild, not a re-read.</p>
    <blockquote>None of them is a wrong answer. Each is a correct answer to a question nobody asked.</blockquote>
    <p class="cap">Which is why every gate added here is <b>verified in both directions</b> &mdash; silent
      on the real data, and made to fail on a fault planted on purpose. A check that has only ever passed
      has not been shown to be a check.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="relationship-not-person">Ask the index for a relationship, not for a person</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Nearly every genealogical search is a search for somebody you can already name. You type
      <em>Hendrik Stefanus Booijzen, born about 1827</em>, and the index tells you whether it holds
      him. That works, and it is how most of this archive was built. But it has a hard ceiling:
      <b>it can only ever confirm what you already suspected.</b> If the person you need was never
      written down by your family, you cannot type their name, and so you cannot find them.</p>
    <p>The search forms will also take a <b>relationship</b>. Leave the forename blank, leave the
      district blank, and ask instead for <em>any child whose mother's surname was Kolbe</em>.
      The index then answers a question nobody in the family was in a position to ask.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:13rem">What you ask for</th><th style="width:12rem">What it can return</th><th>Ceiling</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>A person</b><br /><span class="m">givenName + surname + date</span></td>
              <td>The record of someone you named</td>
              <td>You must already know the name. Everyone your family forgot stays forgotten.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>A relationship</b><br /><span class="m">motherSurname, fatherGivenName, spouseSurname</span></td>
              <td>Anyone standing in that relation, <b>including people you could not have named</b></td>
              <td>Noisy, and every hit needs separating from the namesakes by hand.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><b>Both results on this page's hardest day came out of that difference.</b> Three
      person-searches for <a href={u("/who/elizabetha-van-der-merwe/")}>Elizabetha van der Merwe</a>
      returned nothing, and the third of them — her son's own baptism — was the best test this archive
      could devise. One relationship-search on the Barrys returned
      <a href={u("/barry/#the-missing-two")}>two children on neither parent's death notice</a>. The
      difference was not effort or luck. It was which question the form was asked.</p>
    <blockquote><b>The same logic runs underneath three other things this archive did.</b> Reading a
      <a href={u("/documents/#estate-7081")}>marriage register's Remarks column</a> for the Master's
      estate number is a relationship search done on paper — it indexes from a living remarrying
      widower to a file about a dead first wife. Reading <a href={u("/documents/#kolbe-1291")}>both
      parents' death notices</a> rather than one gave a ninth child. Reading a whole surname out of a
      probate collection, instead of searching it for a person, gave twenty-nine.</blockquote>
    <p><b>The cost is honesty about noise.</b> A relationship search returns the right family mixed
      with strangers, and it returns the same event indexed twice to two different villages. The two
      Barry names above are recorded as a <span class="chip open">lead</span> and not as children,
      because an index line that fits a gap is not the same as a register that fills it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What "verified" on the tree does and does not mean</h2>
  <p>Many people in the source tree carry a tick and a star. Those are <b>MyHeritage interface marks</b> —
    confirmed Smart Matches and direct-ancestor flags. <b>They are not source citations</b>, and this
    archive does not treat them as evidence. A Smart Match means another user's tree agrees; it does not
    mean a register does.</p>
  <p>Where this archive says <span class="chip doc">Documented</span>, there is a named record behind it,
    listed on <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>On quotation</h2>
  <p>The family's own testimony is quoted in full, because it is the family's and because paraphrasing it
    would smooth away exactly the details that make it testable. A long passage on Kitchener's clearance
    policy also sits in the source tree, copied there from a published history of the war; that passage is
    <b>summarised and not reproduced</b>, and its presence is reported as evidence of what the family
    believed rather than as evidence of what happened to them.
    <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="evidence-dropped">The page must not be able to say less than the archive knows</h2>
  <p class="dek">On <b>17 September 2026</b> this archive was asked to look for a fault a sibling
    archive had just had: evidence kept in files keyed to a person, and a build script that never read
    them, so a person documented several times over got a page saying nothing had been read about them.
    <b>The same fault was live here, by a different route, and had been for at least three days.</b></p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>Seventeen people had a page that said &ldquo;This person is recorded in the archive&rsquo;s
      index but is not yet discussed on any page.&rdquo;</b> Among them the <b>1820 Settler</b>, whose
      name the built site carries <b>155 times</b>; <b>John Barry</b>, 158; <b>George Mountjoy</b>, 140;
      <b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b>, 110; <b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b>, 89.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What went wrong</h3>
      <p>The gatherer that collects every mention of a person dropped any name that was a
        <b>strict prefix of a longer person&rsquo;s name</b>. The rule was written for a good reason
        &mdash; to stop <em>John Barry</em> collecting every mention of <em>John Barry Booyzen</em>
        &mdash; and it was implemented as <b>&ldquo;delete the shorter man&rdquo;</b> rather than
        &ldquo;let the longer name go first&rdquo;.</p>
      <p class="cap">So the moment anybody added <em>George Augustus Kolbe of Wakkerstroom</em>,
        <b>George Augustus Kolbe stopped existing</b> as far as the build was concerned.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Why it is worth a section</h3>
      <p><b>The data was never wrong.</b> Every record, every document, every citation was intact and
        on disk the whole time. What failed was the machinery between the evidence and the page, and it
        failed <b>silently and in the safe-sounding direction</b> &mdash; the page did not print a
        falsehood about a document, it printed a falsehood about the archive.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><b>That is the worst kind for a site like this one</b>,
        whose whole claim is that it says what it has read and what it has not.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><b>The rule that comes out of it.</b> A page may say
    &ldquo;nothing is known&rdquo; only when nothing is known &mdash; never because a build step lost
    it. So the check that now runs on every build does not look for the bug that was found; it looks
    for <b>the shape of it</b>: <span class="m">a person the index calls undiscussed whose name the
    built pages plainly carry</span>. Any future gatherer that eats a person trips it, however it eats
    them. It was verified the only way a gate can be &mdash; <b>by putting the bug back and confirming
    the build refused</b>.</p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">Two thousand four hundred and twenty-eight mentions came back.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="index-omits">An index is only honest if something checks it against what it indexes</h2>
  <p class="dek">Three days after the last fault, the same shape again, one layer further out.
    <b>/documents/ builds its own table of contents by matching <span class="m">&lt;h2 id&gt;</span></b>,
    so a heading written without one is left out of the contents <em>and</em> has no anchor for anything
    to link to. <b>Thirty-five had accumulated.</b></p>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p>Among them: <b>the 1842 will of a Bond Street tailor</b>; <b>the sworn falsehood in Shoreditch
      in 1819</b>; <b>the oldest document in this archive</b>; <b>the grave at Burgersdorp</b>; the two
      1845 documents that corrected the family tree twice over. <b>Full write-ups of documents, every
      one, and not one of them listed or linkable.</b></p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px">Nothing was broken. <b>The page rendered perfectly</b>, every section was
    there to a reader scrolling past, and the link checker was satisfied &mdash; no link pointed at a
    missing anchor, because <b>there were no links to point at them at all</b>. That is the whole
    difficulty. <b>The fault was an absence, and an absence is invisible from inside the thing that has
    it.</b> It is the same lesson as the seventeen silent people above, and as the three letters that
    were out in the world with no work-list row watching for a reply.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>The rule this archive now works to:</b> wherever the build derives one
    thing from another &mdash; a contents list from headings, a dossier from pages, a work list from the
    letters on disk &mdash; <b>something must count the two against each other and refuse when they
    disagree</b>. A derived index that nothing checks will drift, and it will look right the entire time
    it is doing it.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><b>The gate.</b> <span class="m">checkregisters.py</span>
    check 13 refuses the build when a page that builds a contents list from its headings carries a
    heading with no id. <b>Verified in both directions</b>: with all thirty-six ids in place it passes
    with nothing failing; with two taken out again it names both headings and exits 1.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Sibling archives</h2>
  <p class="dek">Same method, same author: <b>The Defranceski</b> (Istria), <b>The Falco</b> (Arienzo,
    Campania), <b>The Lerena</b> (Rosario, the Cape, Brisbane). The marriage of
    <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</b> to <b>Nuno Fernando Lerena</b> at Mayfair in 1949 is the hinge
    between this archive and the Lerena one.</p>
</section>
</Base>
